@page "/compare/client-auth"
@using TrustCartographer.UI.Shared

<PageTitle>Client Auth: UDAP vs vLEI — TrustCartographer</PageTitle>

<div class="page-header">
    <FluentLabel Typo="Typography.PageTitle">Client trust over the same OAuth2/FHIR flow</FluentLabel>
    <div class="page-subtitle">
        A payer's FHIR client authenticates to another payer's token endpoint and pulls a <code>Patient</code>.
        Flip the client-auth mode: the <strong>only</strong> things that change are the
        <strong>client assertion</strong> the client presents and <strong>how the Authorization Server
        establishes trust</strong> in it. Everything else — discovery, the OAuth2 token exchange, the access
        token, the FHIR call — is identical. That is the VITAL white paper's claim that vLEI/ACDC client auth
        is <em>semantically equivalent</em> to UDAP JWT client auth (SSRAA / Facilitated FHIR).
        <span class="muted">Conceptual walkthrough (not live traffic). See
        <code>docs/comparisons/clientauth-comparison-sketch.md</code>.</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="control-bar">
    <span style="font-weight:600; margin-right:0.25rem;">Client auth:</span>
    <FluentButton Appearance="@(mode == AuthMode.Udap ? Appearance.Accent : Appearance.Outline)"
                  Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => SetMode(AuthMode.Udap))">
        UDAP JWT (x5c)
    </FluentButton>
    <FluentButton Appearance="@(mode == AuthMode.Vlei ? Appearance.Accent : Appearance.Outline)"
                  Size="ButtonSize.Small" OnClick="@(() => SetMode(AuthMode.Vlei))">
        vLEI / ACDC (IPEX)
    </FluentButton>
    <span style="width:1.25rem;"></span>
    <FluentButton Appearance="Appearance.Accent" Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                  Disabled="@isPlaying" OnClick="PlayAll"
                  IconStart="@(new RegularIcons.Size16.Play())">
        @(isPlaying ? "Playing…" : "Play")
    </FluentButton>
    <FluentButton Appearance="Appearance.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                  Disabled="@(isPlaying || currentIndex >= steps.Count - 1)" OnClick="StepForward">
        Step
    </FluentButton>
    <FluentButton Appearance="Appearance.Outline" Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                  Disabled="@isPlaying" OnClick="Replay"
                  IconStart="@(new RegularIcons.Size16.ArrowCounterclockwise())">
        Replay
    </FluentButton>
</div>

<FluentCard Style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
    <strong>Same vs different</strong>
    <div style="font-size:0.85rem; margin-top:0.4rem;">
        ✅ Same: discovery · <code>POST /token</code> (<code>grant_type=client_credentials</code>) · access token · <code>GET /Patient</code><br />
        ◆ Differs: the <strong>client assertion</strong> (step 2) and the <strong>AS trust validation</strong> (step 4).
        Steps marked ◆ below change with the toggle.
    </div>
</FluentCard>

<div class="diagram-wrap">
    <SsiSequenceDiagram Actors="@actors" Steps="@steps" CurrentIndex="@currentIndex"
                        SelectedStepIndex="@selectedStepIndex"
                        Complete="@isComplete" Success="true"
                        SuccessLabel="FHIR DATA RETURNED" FailLabel="DENIED" />
</div>

@if (currentIndex >= 0)
{
    <div class="step-list">
        @for (var i = 0; i <= currentIndex && i < steps.Count; i++)
        {
            var idx = i;
            var step = steps[idx];
            var sel = selectedStepIndex == idx;
            <div class="step-item @(sel ? "step-item-selected" : "")" @onclick="@(() => SelectStep(idx))">
                <div class="step-item-header">
                    <span class="step-number">@(idx + 1)</span>
                    <span class="step-label">@step.Label</span>
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(step.HttpMethod))
                    {
                        <span class="step-http">@step.HttpMethod @step.HttpPath</span>
                    }
                </div>
                @if (sel)
                {
                    <div class="step-item-body">@((MarkupString)explanations[idx])</div>
                }
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

<FluentCard Style="margin-top: 1rem;">
    <strong>Mapping</strong>
    <table class="cmp">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>UDAP (SSRAA)</th><th>vLEI / ACDC</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Client assertion</td><td><code>private_key_jwt</code> (JOSE <code>x5c</code>)</td><td>CESR-encoded <strong>IPEX Grant</strong> (ACDC chain)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Identity anchor</td><td>X.509 SAN URI → <strong>trust community CA</strong></td><td>KERI AID → <strong>GLEIF root</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purpose-of-use</td><td><code>hl7-b2b.purpose_of_use</code></td><td><strong>ECR</strong> credential</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Registration trust</td><td>software statement + cert chain</td><td>QVI-issued credential chain</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Governance</td><td>Trust Community</td><td>vLEI EGF (GLEIF→QVI)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.8rem;">
        Reframe: this isn't only "SSI vs UDAP" — there is a <strong>UDAP vLEI/ACDC Client Authentication</strong>
        profile the white paper calls semantically equivalent to UDAP JWT client auth, differing only in the
        assertion format. Both also answer TrustCartographer's deliberate "verifier has no trust list" hook —
        with a community CA vs the GLEIF root.
    </p>
</FluentCard>

<style>
    .cmp { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
    .cmp th, .cmp td { text-align: left; padding: 0.35rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(127,127,127,0.2); vertical-align: top; }
    .cmp th { font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; opacity: 0.75; }
    .cmp td:first-child { opacity: 0.7; white-space: nowrap; }
    .muted { opacity: 0.7; }
</style>
